From: Justin Casey <4348807+justincasey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 15:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] DeepSeek V4.1 CED: one decoder row for a prefill chunk
 nothing reads again

A step's CED decoder (layers from the full-resolution KV source on) runs on
each request's last 128 rows, and later steps read those rows' decoder state
only through 128-row windows: the next chunk's kept rows replay from inside
that chunk once it has more than 128 rows, decode rows read the last 128
positions, and the DSpark drafter reads back from a draft's first position by
its SWA index width. With the scheduler's aligned prefill chunks
(ALIGN_PREFILL_CHUNKS, the TileLang family), a non-final chunk that ends on a
long_prefill_token_threshold boundary is followed by min(remaining, threshold)
rows, so when at least max(window, DSpark SWA index width) + 1 + the step's
sampled and draft rows remain (200 at k=6), no later decoder or draft window
reaches its decoder rows, and its logits are discarded (a chunked prefill
samples no token). Such a chunk now keeps one decoder row, its last, instead
of 128; its global KV and index keys are still built from every row at the
boundary layer. Every token the request commits is computed from the same
values as before; other requests in the step see the same inputs, and the
same bits where the decoder kernels do not depend on the step's row count.

plan_ced and CEDState.stage take per-request kept-row limits
(nonfinal_limits); the plan's max_query_len keeps the window bound, so the
decoder runs in the same mode and plans as without the limit. Chunks that are
not truncated anyway, prompt-logprobs requests, multimodal requests (encoder
budgets can cut their next chunk short) and other drafters keep their rows. A
skipped chunk's end becomes the request's replay floor for its later steps,
so even a later chunk the scheduler shortened cannot read the skipped rows.
DSV41_CED_SKIP_NONFINAL=0 turns it off.

The CED decoder and its replay start are Local Inference Lab's (Luke Alonso)
and Christopher Owen's (spark-ds41f) code; the bounded decoder replay follows
the idea of vllm-project/vllm#58132 (ivanium) and #59894 (gcanlin). No
upstream code is ported.

License: Apache-2.0 (dsv41-1017 recipe, DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017-4x-DGX-Spark).
